# The SDK is pinned to OUR FORK, not to Free-Ink/freeink-sdk.
#
# The firmware needs work that is not on upstream main: the LilyGo T5 S3 board
# support of Free-Ink/freeink-sdk#51 (still open), our fixes on top of it, and
# the borrowed panel canvas behind native multi-level grayscale. A build against
# upstream main does not compile -- HalDisplay alone loses supportsGrayFrame(),
# grayLevels(), borrowGray8Canvas() and displayGray8Canvas().
#
# This used to name the upstream URL, and it resolved anyway: GitHub shares an
# object store across a fork network, so fetching our fork-only commit by its
# exact SHA succeeds even through the upstream URL. That is grace, not
# configuration -- it would end the day the fork left the network, and the
# failure would surface as an unexplained CI break. Naming the fork makes the
# pin say what it means.
#
# `branch` is `witchhunt`, the fork's one long-lived integration branch, and it
# stays that name. Upstream resyncs are MERGED into it and our SDK fixes land on
# it; it is never rebased or force-pushed, so every SHA this file has ever
# pinned stays reachable from it (the one older pin no branch contained is kept
# by the tag archive/pin-feddc4e86). The pin may trail the branch, never leave
# it, and CI enforces that (scripts/check_sdk_pin.sh): land SDK commits on the
# branch first, then pin them. Upstream PR branches are still cut from Free-Ink
# main and merged back here.
#
# The name only matters to `git submodule update --remote`, which is NOT what a
# normal checkout runs -- but when it was `main`, that command silently reset
# the submodule to upstream and the firmware then failed to build.
[submodule "freeink-sdk"]
	path = freeink-sdk
	url = https://github.com/jpirnay/freeink-sdk.git
	branch = witchhunt
